  "analysis": "We have to solve a tree problem: find the maximum length of a consecutive label interval [l, r] such that there exists a connected subtree of size at most k that contains all vertices with labels in [l, r]. In a tree, the minimal connected subtree containing a set S of vertices is the union of paths between them (the Steiner tree). Its size equals (#edges in the Steiner tree) + 1. A known trick: for a set of nodes, sort them by tin (Euler tour entry times) and consider the sum f of distances between consecutive nodes in this cyclic order (including last to first). Then the size of the minimal Steiner tree is f/2 + 1. We can maintain f dynamically as we insert/remove vertices in the set S using a balanced BST ordered by tin. This allows a two-pointer approach over intervals [l, r] and keeps the total complexity ~ O(n log n).

